00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.820
- And since you're the one who screwed me,

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.163
I'd say you owe me one.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.706
- Technically, he screwed his wife.

00:00:10.706 --> 00:00:13.373
(playful music)

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:17.430
Hey legal eagles, it's
time to think like lawyer.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:20.620
You asked for it so
more analysis of Suits.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.470
Today we are covering the second episode

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:23.400
in the first season.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.320
I think I will keep going
with the first season,

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.070
but if there are specific
episodes later on in the series

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.920
that are especially
ripe for legal analysis,

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.350
please let me know and
maybe I'll jump ahead.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.150
As always, of course please subscribe

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.870
and comment in the form of an objection,

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.520
which I will either sustain or overrule.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.360
And of course stick around
until the end of this video

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.470
where I give Suits episode
two a grade for legal realism.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.230
So without further ado, let's dig in

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:50.871
to the second episode of Suits.

00:00:50.871 --> 00:00:52.950
- Come on, what do you say,
we go five out of nine?

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:54.300
- So you can keep abusing me?

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.030
No thank you, I'm done.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.940
- Oh come on, ill spot you three.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.700
- Game time's over, Wyatt, they're here.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:00.720
- They're here?

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:01.946
- Get set up in the conference room.

00:01:01.946 --> 00:01:03.320
- Yeah, no I'll just uh.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.043
- Get set up in the conference room.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:08.070
- I have never seen a law office

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.750
that has a game room like
that, even in New York

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:14.730
where law offices for big
firms can be pretty nice.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.680
They don't really look
anything like the kind of

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.580
Silicon Valley start-up
offices that you see

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.790
often on TV, so this is not a particularly

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:25.371
realistic depiction of
what a law firm looks like.

00:01:25.371 --> 00:01:26.970
- Wait, wait, wait, where
do you think you're going?

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.040
- Into the room with the people.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.327
- Wrong, that's the adult table in there,

00:01:31.327 --> 00:01:32.833
and you haven't earned the privilege yet.

00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:34.690
- But, I, I played air hockey with you.

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:35.730
- You need to go back to the office

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:36.960
and file a patent for the phone.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.472
- A patent, I don't know
how to file a patent.

00:01:38.472 --> 00:01:39.305
- What?

00:01:39.305 --> 00:01:40.138
- Figure it out.

00:01:40.138 --> 00:01:40.971
- Can't we do that after--

00:01:40.971 --> 00:01:42.000
- No.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.430
- So that would be crazy
to wait to file a patent

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:49.410
for this technology that you're
trying to get funding for.

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:51.330
Effectively what's happening here is that

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:53.550
they're going to publish the technology

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.380
that they want these people to invest in

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:56.960
before they have protected it.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.550
We are only about a minute
and a half into this episode

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:02.140
and this is already
malpractice that would probably

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:03.630
get these guys disbarred.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.440
On top of that, this
meeting is only gonna last

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.270
a couple of hours and
it's always a good idea

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.650
to make sure your entire
legal team is up to date

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:11.890
with all of the information
that's going back and forth.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.140
So if this is a big investment deal,

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.750
Mike should be in there to
learn the facts on the ground,

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:20.450
and he should have filed his
patent months if not years

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:22.533
ahead of this meeting, this is crazy.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:25.680
- Okay have you ever
filed a patent before?

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.300
I just got back from
this meeting with Harvey

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:28.487
and he wants me to file this patent,

00:02:28.487 --> 00:02:30.610
but I have no idea what
that paperwork looks like,

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.507
so any help in this
arena would be really--

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.060
- Isn't Mike supposed to
be a genius of the law?

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.130
Isn't that why they hired him?

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.760
He's like the greatest
legal mind of his generation

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.350
even though he doesn't have a law degree?

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:45.020
And yet he's constantly
going back to his paralegal

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:48.830
for information about the most
basic things that lawyers do?

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:50.725
I sense some inconsistencies

00:02:50.725 --> 00:02:53.724
in this show and the writing so far.

00:02:53.724 --> 00:02:55.910
(tense music)

00:02:55.910 --> 00:03:00.630
- Mike Ross, allow me to
introduce the Bainbridge briefs.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:01.463
- Which stack?

00:03:01.463 --> 00:03:02.296
- All of them.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:04.860
- Wait a minute, are these all--

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:07.060
- Still printing, yeah, I
give it about a half hour.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:10.720
- Oh, it's six printers,
23 pages a minute,

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.680
30 minutes, that's 4,140 pages.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.220
- Plus all of this, which means
the next time you negotiate

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:19.140
a deal, I would suggest to
get your facts straight.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.080
- In reality, if you
have gone to the printer

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.570
with all of your briefs,
all those briefs have been

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:28.400
proofed probably a dozen
times by 10 different people

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.190
in a big law firm like this.

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:32.500
You would never go to the
trouble of printing out

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.520
thousands and thousands
of pages of documents

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:37.560
unless you were 100% certain
that all of those documents

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.610
were ready to be filed with the court.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:41.960
But what is accurate here,
and is kind of amusing,

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:44.510
is the way that they bind
all of these documents.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:47.260
It's called Velo binding,
it's those sort of plastic

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:50.010
jaws that you open up,
you have a special machine

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:53.220
that opens them up and then
you place the briefs in them

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.303
and then you close them up on top of them.

00:03:55.303 --> 00:03:58.480
That is, for certain courts
exactly how you have to

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.390
submit your briefs, it's
a real pain in the butt.

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:04.190
So at least that attention
to detail is fairly accurate.

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:07.260
- Please tell me that you've filed the--

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:09.134
- Patent, no, not yet.

00:04:09.134 --> 00:04:10.610
- I gave it to you yesterday.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:12.460
- Same time I gave you
my Bainbridge briefs,

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:13.460
which you haven't finished.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:15.500
- I was here all night and
I barely got through half.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.130
- I was on hold for
almost 11 minutes before

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.720
I gave up on your patent,
so what's your point?

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:21.170
- All right, let's talk a little bit

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:23.437
about what it means to file a patent.

00:04:23.437 --> 00:04:26.987
The paperwork to file a patent
is not particularly complex.

00:04:26.987 --> 00:04:30.840
There are forms that you
can use, it's not that hard.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.160
The problem is that filing for a patent

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.000
is not just filing for a patent,

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:36.790
it's all the work that goes into filing

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:38.250
for the patent before you do it.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.740
If there is prior art,
meaning that there is someone

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.280
who filed a similar patent before yours,

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:47.530
or that your idea has already
been taken somewhere else,

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:49.450
then your patent will be invalid.

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:51.310
So when you go to a law firm and ask them

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:53.590
to file a patent on your behalf,

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:55.660
you're not just asking
them to file the actual,

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:57.410
physical paperwork for the patent,

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:01.110
what you're asking them to do
is hundreds of hours of work

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:04.240
where attorneys will
cull through the database

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:06.800
that contains all of the
other prior applications,

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.210
some of which have been denied,

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:09.380
some of which have been approved,

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:11.980
and they're looking for similar things

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:14.400
that might invalidate your own patent.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.690
And in fact, if you're following politics,

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:19.260
you might know that our
acting Attorney General,

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:20.760
Matt Whitaker was involved with a company

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.930
called World Wide Marketing,
which was an infamous

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:27.740
patent scam that was known
for filing patents without

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.120
doing the leg work to find
out if there was prior art.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:32.980
So a lot of people who paid
tens of thousands of dollars

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:36.310
to this company in order
to get the patents approved

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:38.180
found that their patents
were actually invalidated

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:40.840
because this company wasn't
doing a prior art search.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.070
So it's crazy, number one,
that they haven't filed

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:45.640
a patent for this thing before
they've gone to investors,

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:47.890
and number two, it's crazy
that they think it's just

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:50.460
a simple matter of filing
for the patent itself

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.190
when in fact it's the hundreds
if not thousands of hours

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:56.700
it takes for the leg work
before you file for that patent.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:57.850
- You wanted to see me?

00:06:01.633 --> 00:06:02.466
- Sit.

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:12.583
I looked through your work
on the Bainbridge briefs.

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:18.283
Spectacular.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:20.530
- It's just proofing.

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:23.220
How could proofing
something be spectacular?

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:24.410
- Sup?

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.014
- We just heard from Wyatt.

00:06:26.014 --> 00:06:28.113
We got a response from the patent office.

00:06:29.163 --> 00:06:32.010
- You got a response back
from the patent office

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:33.890
a day after you filed your patent?

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:36.840
That, uh, that does not happen. (chuckles)

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:37.673
- And?

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:40.460
- It's been denied.

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:41.380
- What?

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:43.458
- Evidently there was a similar claim.

00:06:43.458 --> 00:06:45.250
- Wait, what do you mean,
somebody beat us to it?

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.287
- No, someone beat you to it.

00:06:47.287 --> 00:06:49.430
You filed less than 24 hours ago,

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:52.380
which means you filed a day
later than you said you would.

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:54.760
- Oh my god, well yeah,

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:57.980
that's why you file for a
patent months if not years

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.760
before you start getting
investments from outside people,

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:02.920
that's the problem with going to investors

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:03.910
before you've gotten the patent.

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:05.510
This whole episode is so stupid.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:08.013
- Okay, what's gonna happen now?

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.660
- I suggest you get on the
phone, call the patent office,

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:13.050
and find out who beat us to it.

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:15.150
Then you're gonna have
Donna find any judge

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:17.110
who will listen so we
can get an injunction

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:19.570
and stop whoever it is from
launching their product first.

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:20.403
- Okay.

00:07:20.403 --> 00:07:21.890
- An injunction on what grounds?

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:23.910
It's true that sometimes you can seek out

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:25.850
the extraordinary relief
from a court, an injunction,

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:28.440
which is basically a court
order to prevent someone

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.860
from doing something or it
forces them to do something,

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:32.770
but you have to have
legal grounds to do it.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:35.170
And the fact that someone filed a patent

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:37.520
based on the information
that you provided to them

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:39.640
is not grounds for an injunction
unless there was some sort

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:42.310
of non-disclosure agreement
at the investors' meeting,

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:44.750
but that seems relatively unlikely.

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:47.793
We'll see, not a good legal tactic so far.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:50.273
- Hey, Harvey.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:53.663
Did you tell him it was me?

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:56.043
- Why would I do that?

00:07:56.043 --> 00:07:58.380
I'm responsible for you, it was me.

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:00.347
- Yeah, and that would be true whether

00:08:00.347 --> 00:08:02.790
Mike was actually an attorney or not,

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:04.470
which he is not in this case.

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:05.860
Harvey is the lead attorney,

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:08.140
he's the one that's
signing papers most likely,

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:10.460
and so it's his neck on the line.

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:12.810
Also, by the way, it was his decision

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:16.870
not to file for a patent until
after he met with investors.

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:20.421
That is malpractice, he'd get
totally disbarred for that.

00:08:20.421 --> 00:08:22.220
(tense music)

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:24.300
- [Judge] Did you not
see the sign outside?

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:25.330
- Just putting it away, your Honor.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:27.220
- It would already be in your pocket,

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:30.120
but you are running late, so
perhaps that's why it wasn't.

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:33.410
Let the record so that
council is fined $1,000

00:08:33.410 --> 00:08:35.300
for failing to follow the
posted rules of the court.

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:36.259
- Your Honor, I didn't mean--

00:08:36.259 --> 00:08:37.802
- And for mouthing off.

00:08:37.802 --> 00:08:38.800
- Mouthing off?

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:42.731
- Yeah, so that's a bit
extreme, but I have seen judges

00:08:42.731 --> 00:08:45.090
throw attorneys out of their court room

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:47.620
for cell phones whose ringers go off.

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:50.580
I've never seen a judge
chastise council for just

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:53.200
displaying a cell phone
that's not making any noise,

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:54.800
but if the rules of the court say that

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.130
you can't have a cell
phone in that courtroom,

00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:59.620
then you can't have a cell
phone in that courtroom.

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:03.140
The judge gets to decide what
goes on in his or her court,

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:05.530
and there are plenty of
courts that don't even

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:08.140
let you take a cell phone
into the building itself,

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:09.650
that is the case with a lot of the federal

00:09:09.650 --> 00:09:13.600
courthouses in Virginia and
Washington DC where I practice.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:16.120
So you really have to be
careful and know the rules

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:18.250
of the court before you
head in to the courthouse.

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:19.610
- Request denied.
(gavel bangs)

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:21.210
Court adjourned for the morning.

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:24.283
Better luck next time, Harvey.

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:30.707
- From now on, I want him
when I go against you.

00:09:30.707 --> 00:09:32.790
(laughs)

00:09:34.104 --> 00:09:34.937
- [Woman] Your Honor, I have--

00:09:34.937 --> 00:09:35.883
- [Judge] Send them in.

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:39.830
- Oh, no no no no no.

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:42.510
You do not get to have a
private meeting with the judge

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:44.380
without opposing council present.

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:47.770
This is an ex parte communication,
this is totally improper.

00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:50.200
Even though the judge has
already denied this request,

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:51.720
there might be an appeal, there might be

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:53.540
other issues in this particular case.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:55.790
This is totally improper
to meet with one side

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:58.340
and not the other, this
should never happen.

00:09:58.340 --> 00:09:59.730
- I could have you brought up on review

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:00.890
for talking to me like that.

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:02.830
- Then we can get it all on record.

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:04.030
- Get what?

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:06.520
- Whatever it is that you
seem to have against me

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:07.740
for no apparent reason.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:10.240
I have a solid argument
for any reasonable judge

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:13.280
to grant my injunction, so
solid it begs the question

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:15.190
why do you have it in for me?

00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:17.410
And I don't even know your first name.

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:20.220
- Really, my wife never mentioned it?

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:21.570
- Your wife?

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:24.130
- The woman you had an
affair with last month?

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:26.950
- Ah okay, so that is
what we call a classic

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:28.370
conflict of interest.

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:31.530
In California, what you
would do is file a 170.6,

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:35.100
which is a request for the
judge to recuse themselves.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:37.781
There is no possible way that this judge

00:10:37.781 --> 00:10:40.330
gets to preside over this particular case

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:42.982
when we has a huge bias problem.

00:10:42.982 --> 00:10:45.350
Now that Harvey knows this information,

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:48.000
he absolutely should bring
this judge up on review.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:49.190
The damage has already been done, though,

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:51.334
the judge has issued his ruling,

00:10:51.334 --> 00:10:53.715
so he can't back out now.

00:10:53.715 --> 00:10:56.360
There it isn't going to be a
good outcome for this judge,

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:58.520
regardless of what unscrupulous conduct

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:01.273
Harvey has engaged in
here, this is not good.

00:11:01.273 --> 00:11:04.273
(upbeat rock music)

00:11:08.510 --> 00:11:11.723
God, he's terrible at tennis.

00:11:12.644 --> 00:11:15.713
♪ Waving it around ♪

00:11:15.713 --> 00:11:18.170
- They're both terrible at tennis.

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:20.200
- My earlier ruling wasn't
clear enough for you?

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:22.860
- Crystal, but I didn't
want you suffer for it.

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:24.859
You see, if you don't sign my injunction,

00:11:24.859 --> 00:11:26.540
there's gonna be a law suit.

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:28.769
When the next judge sees all the facts,

00:11:28.769 --> 00:11:30.580
he's gonna overturn your ruling.

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:32.090
- You may be right, but by then

00:11:32.090 --> 00:11:33.350
your clients will have dumped you.

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:35.840
- You may be right, but
that sterling reputation

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:39.030
that you value so highly, down the tubes.

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:41.290
- Oh boy, okay, so this
is called blackmail.

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:43.690
This is incredibly
unethical, not to mention

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:46.170
this is yet another ex
parte communication,

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:47.740
which is totally improper.

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:50.500
Man, I mean, at this point
Harvey's gonna get disbarred,

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:53.170
the judge might be disbarred
and removed from the bench

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:54.840
if this ever comes to light.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:57.560
This is violating your
oath as an attorney.

00:11:57.560 --> 00:11:59.225
- You filing for divorce?

00:11:59.225 --> 00:12:00.133
- Soon enough.

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:06.460
Perhaps we could discuss a
little quid pro quo before I do.

00:12:06.712 --> 00:12:07.916
- I'm listening.

00:12:07.916 --> 00:12:11.560
- You sign a document that
says you slept with my wife,

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:12.950
I'll give you your injunction.

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:14.520
- And why would you want me to do that?

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:15.750
- To prevent her from taking me

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:17.920
for half of everything I own.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:20.920
- So what you're saying is
you'd like to blackmail me.

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:23.730
- [Judge] I'm saying
we could both benefit.

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:25.190
- You're blackmailing each other.

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:27.870
He just came in there and
tried to blackmail the judge

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:30.100
into giving him a verdict that he wanted,

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:32.360
and now the judge is trying
to blackmail him right back.

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:34.862
I mean, these two deserve each other.

00:12:34.862 --> 00:12:37.098
- And since you're the one who screwed me,

00:12:37.098 --> 00:12:38.853
I'd say you owe me one.

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:41.690
- Technically, he screwed his wife.

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:43.810
- And he did the snapping
thing and Gregory--

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:44.643
- Look at me.

00:12:47.114 --> 00:12:49.781
(clears throat)

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:52.633
You're high.

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:57.300
Get out.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:59.260
- Oh that is not a good sign.

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:00.540
- Look, this is not my fault, all right,

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:01.520
Louis made me do it.

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:03.580
- Louis did, right, he
put a gun to your head,

00:13:03.580 --> 00:13:04.510
made you smoke pot.

00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:06.770
- Yeah he did, he pulled
out the drug test,

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:08.050
which I failed by the way,

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:09.770
and then he told me that
if I didn't smoke pot

00:13:09.770 --> 00:13:12.370
to help him land this new
client, that he'd fire me.

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:16.790
It's not so different that asking someone

00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:17.800
out for drinks, is it?

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:18.910
- You and I had a deal.

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:20.157
- I'm sorry.

00:13:20.157 --> 00:13:22.570
- [Harvey] And if next time,
Louis asks you to do something

00:13:22.570 --> 00:13:25.090
that I told you not to do, what then?

00:13:25.090 --> 00:13:27.070
- [Mike] I told you that
I did not have a choice.

00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:28.283
- Oh, because he had a gun to your head.

00:13:28.283 --> 00:13:29.116
- [Mike] Yes.

00:13:29.116 --> 00:13:29.949
- And what are your choices

00:13:29.949 --> 00:13:31.149
if someone puts a gun to your head?

00:13:31.149 --> 00:13:31.982
- What are you talking about?

00:13:31.982 --> 00:13:33.390
You do what they say or they shoot you.

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:36.130
- Wrong, you take the gun
or you pull out a bigger one

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:38.500
or you call their bluff, or you do any one

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:40.772
of 146 other things.

00:13:40.772 --> 00:13:42.480
- This is a really interesting situation.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:46.000
I think that Louis might be liable for

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:50.220
conspiracy to engage in
illegal drug consumption.

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:52.780
Obviously Mike is guilty of having

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:54.270
consumed an illicit substance,

00:13:54.270 --> 00:13:57.340
in New York it's still
illegal to consume marijuana,

00:13:57.340 --> 00:14:01.010
but he was induced to do so by Louis,

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:05.360
they engaged in an agreement
for him to engage in this

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:09.530
particular action for the gain
of both of those individuals.

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:12.130
So I think that given that
there is an agreement,

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:14.850
there is an overt act to further the ends

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:18.440
of the conspiracy here,
I think Louis may in fact

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:22.490
also be guilty of the object
of the conspiracy itself,

00:14:22.490 --> 00:14:25.250
so Louis is as culpable as Mike

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:27.960
for engaging in smoking marijuana.

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:30.214
Interesting legal situation.

00:14:30.214 --> 00:14:31.550
- Do you know how long it was

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:33.500
before I got to sit at the adult table?

00:14:34.505 --> 00:14:36.640
It was when I brought in my first client,

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:39.203
which I don't recall you having done.

00:14:39.203 --> 00:14:41.000
And when you screwed up that patent

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:44.150
and Wyatt went apeshit on
me, I didn't put that on you,

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:45.490
I took it on myself.

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:48.120
- I mean, you both royally
screwed up that patent,

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:49.560
that's not just Mike.

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:52.486
- [Woman] Louis told me
what you did at the club.

00:14:52.486 --> 00:14:54.986
- [Mike] I bet he didn't
tell you the whole story.

00:14:56.920 --> 00:14:58.470
- Told me enough to impress me.

00:14:59.950 --> 00:15:01.303
- He landed the client.

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:04.106
- New business is hard.

00:15:04.106 --> 00:15:05.950
People will promise you the world,

00:15:05.950 --> 00:15:09.203
but until they sign that
engagement letter, means nothing.

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:14.660
I don't know what you told Tom Keller,

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:17.209
but bringing a client in at your age,

00:15:17.209 --> 00:15:18.973
that reminds me of Harvey.

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:25.300
- That's true, it is very
unusual for junior associates

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:28.270
to bring in new clients,
especially potentially

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:30.830
a summer associate like
Mike, it's unclear to me

00:15:30.830 --> 00:15:33.300
whether he's a summer associate
or a junior associate.

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:35.570
He was interviewed as a summer associate,

00:15:35.570 --> 00:15:38.520
which is like an internship,
but he is acting like

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:41.740
a junior associate, writers
didn't quite get that one right.

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:44.970
At any rate, it is incredibly
unusual for a first year

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:48.250
junior associate to bring
in a client of any kind,

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:50.670
and that is the way that you get promoted,

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:52.610
you know, almost immediately.

00:15:52.610 --> 00:15:54.550
- What are you showing
us, is this a website?

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:57.110
- Available at the Suntech domain name.

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:59.560
- All the design plans and
calculations have been uploaded.

00:15:59.560 --> 00:16:00.567
- Is this online now?

00:16:00.567 --> 00:16:01.400
- It can be.

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:02.330
- Which means the whole world

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:03.860
will have access to my designs.

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:05.750
- They'll be 10 knock-offs of that phone

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:08.220
before you can catch a cab
back to your headquarters.

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:09.900
- We could file an injunction.

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:12.750
- Not before tomorrow, and
once that technology is out

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:15.230
there, good luck putting that
genie back in the bottle.

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:16.280
- Well then we'll sue.

00:16:16.280 --> 00:16:18.450
- But my client won't have any money.

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:21.040
What he will have is credit
for the initial design,

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:23.738
which after he incorporates
under a different name,

00:16:23.738 --> 00:16:25.840
will be worth a hell of a lot more money

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:28.270
than the $20 million that you're offering.

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:32.043
- Yeah, except that if the
other side's patent is valid,

00:16:32.043 --> 00:16:35.938
then they can sue anyone
who uses those designs

00:16:35.938 --> 00:16:39.460
and builds the prototype
without their permission.

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:41.350
That's the point of the patent protection.

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:43.590
And they would probably be
able to sue this poor gentleman

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:46.050
out of existence for a
whole raft of things,

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:48.410
including intentional
interference with contract,

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:50.320
disclosure of trade secrets,

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:52.240
and still at the end of the day,

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:53.990
these nefarious guys on the other side

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:55.650
still have their patent
because Harvey and Mike

00:16:55.650 --> 00:16:58.290
didn't file their patent
when they should have.

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:01.330
So um, yeah, this is probably yet another

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:03.423
malpractice and disbarment trap.

00:17:06.790 --> 00:17:07.742
- What is this?

00:17:07.742 --> 00:17:10.570
- It's a copy of the
judicial conduct codes.

00:17:10.570 --> 00:17:12.580
Friend of mine who works at
the Attorney General's office

00:17:12.580 --> 00:17:14.750
gave it to me, we had
a nice chat about you.

00:17:14.750 --> 00:17:17.780
I told him if you were willing
to blackmail someone once,

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:20.100
chances are you've done it before.

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:21.730
He's very anxious to meet you.

00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:23.710
- You think you can get
away with screwing my wife

00:17:23.710 --> 00:17:25.180
and then have me investigated?

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:26.860
- You actually have it
the wrong way around.

00:17:26.860 --> 00:17:29.160
The only thing I've done so far

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:30.553
is have you investigated.

00:17:31.820 --> 00:17:32.653
- What?

00:17:32.653 --> 00:17:35.023
- I never actually slept with Lauren,

00:17:35.023 --> 00:17:38.320
but I knew you'd never believe
me so I kept my mouth shut.

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:40.130
But now that she's getting a divorce,

00:17:40.130 --> 00:17:42.969
my policy no longer
applies, and of course,

00:17:42.969 --> 00:17:46.319
she's free to date whoever she pleases.

00:17:46.319 --> 00:17:47.713
And she pleases me.

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:51.810
Enjoy your evening, Donald.

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:53.750
- No honor among thieves.

00:17:53.750 --> 00:17:56.080
The judge should have gotten
him to sign an affidavit

00:17:56.080 --> 00:17:58.160
at the same time that he issued his ruling

00:17:58.160 --> 00:17:59.500
if he really was gonna follow through

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:00.623
with the blackmail here.

00:18:00.623 --> 00:18:04.370
That was just poor lawyering
on the judge's part.

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:07.037
(playful music)

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:10.780
All right, so now it's time
to give Suits episode two

00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:12.945
a grade for legal realism.

00:18:12.945 --> 00:18:15.370
(gavel bangs)

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:17.020
There was some accurate discussion

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:18.910
of what it takes to file for a patent,

00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:21.690
but also a complete disregard
for all of the leg work

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:23.360
that goes into it, and of course,

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:25.340
all of the attorneys in this episode

00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:27.760
would have been disbarred
many, many times over.

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:30.470
Last time around, I gave Suits a B-.

00:18:30.470 --> 00:18:32.630
I'm afraid this episode is
slightly less realistic,

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:35.910
